From e05fc7ff4e2da0419be81771b1639985e8dcea1d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yohan <you@example.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 03:04:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Backend: add fetch timing diagnostics and bounded Gemini
 generation

Regenerated against the current HEAD (feec12e, "Backend: fix ML loading
and add Gemini provider"). Confirmed this repo's actual current
llmClient.ts/aiAssessment.ts before editing (bare, no prior [ai-debug]
logging present in this pushed state).

DIAGNOSIS

fetchWithTimeout() (src/utils/http.ts) was inspected and left
unchanged - it's a standard, correct AbortController timeout pattern
with no defect found. The scan route's Promise.all([assessAi(...),
analyzeInfrastructure(...)]) runs them independently; infra's own
timing can't extend AI's timeout. LLM_TIMEOUT_MS is read fresh per
call and, per the reported behavior (waiting close to the full 60s
before erroring), is being parsed and applied correctly - not a
NaN-immediate-fire situation.

The one concrete, evidenced difference between the working direct
request and the app's real request: the app's request additionally
carries a systemInstruction and a much larger, structurally complex
`contents` payload (subject, up to 4000 chars of body, up to 12 header
anomalies, SPF/DKIM/DMARC, up to 10 URLs/domains, ML fields) - and,
critically, sets no explicit thinking/output bounds at all.

I verified against Google's current Gemini API docs (live search, since
my training data predates Gemini 3) that gemini-3.6-flash is a Gemini
3.x-generation model, which:
  - uses generationConfig.thinkingConfig.thinkingLevel ("low" /
    "medium" / "high") to control reasoning effort - NOT the legacy
    Gemini 2.5-series thinkingBudget field (mixing the two returns an
    HTTP 400)
  - cannot fully disable thinking on the Flash tier - only reduce it
  - defaults to a "medium" thinking level when unspecified (documented
    for the closely related 3.5 Flash tier)
  - reports thinking tokens and output tokens as SEPARATE budgets in
    usage metadata, meaning maxOutputTokens alone does not bound
    reasoning time

This makes "the model's default thinking effort scaling up on a large,
complex prompt while a two-word prompt returns instantly" a plausible,
evidence-grounded explanation - not a proven one. I have no network
egress to generativelanguage.googleapis.com from this sandbox, so I
cannot confirm live that this resolves the timeout. The timing/error
diagnostics added here will make that immediately visible on the next
real run: either a fast, successful response (supporting this
diagnosis), or a fast HTTP 400 if thinkingLevel is ever rejected for
this model (surfaced clearly, not a silent hang), or a genuinely slow
fetch phase (pointing elsewhere).

CHANGES

1. src/services/llmClient.ts (createGeminiProvider):
   - Added generationConfig.maxOutputTokens: 2048, sized against this
     app's own llmOutput.ts schema worst case (summary <=2000 chars +
     recommendedActions <=12x300 chars + attackType <=80 chars ~=
     5.7KB ~= ~1500 tokens, rounded up with margin).
   - Added generationConfig.thinkingConfig: { thinkingLevel: "low" } -
     the Gemini 3.x-appropriate field (see diagnosis above); never
     thinkingBudget (legacy 2.5-series field, would 400 if combined).
   - Added the exact timing checkpoints requested: before fetch / fetch
     started / fetch resolved / response status / before
     response.json() / after response.json(), logging only elapsed ms,
     request body byte length, timeout value, HTTP status, and an
     approximate response body byte length (computed from the already-
     parsed body via JSON.stringify, so existing/new tests' fetch mocks
     don't need a new res.text() capability).
   - Kept the existing safe Gemini-error-message surfacing and
     finishReason/blockReason logging from the prior change.
   - fetchWithTimeout() itself and the LlmProvider interface are
     UNCHANGED.

2. src/analyzers/aiAssessment.ts:
   - Added an elapsed-time log specifically around the
     provider.complete() call (start timestamp immediately before the
     call, elapsed logged immediately after it resolves), plus the
     prior JSON-parse/schema-validation success/failure diagnostics and
     per-stage failure logging (provider.complete() / JSON.parse() /
     Zod validation / unexpected), all content-free.

3. src/scripts/geminiTimingDiagnostic.ts (new, manual-only):
   - Run via `npm run diagnose:gemini` (or
     `npx tsx src/scripts/geminiTimingDiagnostic.ts`).
   - Loads the real .env (dotenv/config) and calls the actual
     llmProviderFromEnv() / LlmProvider.complete() - the same provider
     code the app uses, unchanged interface - once with a tiny prompt,
     once with a synthetic "realistic" prompt built via the real
     buildLlmUserPayload() using placeholder Latin filler text sized to
     match a real scan payload (never real email content).
   - Prints only byte sizes and elapsed milliseconds for a direct
     side-by-side comparison against the user's native-fetch baseline.
   - Not part of `npm test`; uses no API key of its own (reads whatever
     is in the local, gitignored .env; safe to run with none
     configured - prints "provider is null" and exits cleanly, verified
     directly).
   - Added a package.json "diagnose:gemini" script for convenience.

4. tests/llmClient.test.ts: added 3 tests (mocked fetch, no real key) -
   confirms maxOutputTokens and thinkingConfig.thinkingLevel are sent
   and thinkingBudget is never also sent; confirms Gemini's own error
   message is still surfaced; confirms the new timing instrumentation
   doesn't change the resolved value or require anything new from
   existing fetch mocks.

Nothing changed in: fetchWithTimeout(), the LlmProvider interface, the
ML classifier, the risk engine, Batch 5C, Batch 6, or the frontend.

Never logged anywhere in this change: the API key, email body/prompt
text, or the raw LLM response text.

npm test: 247/247 passing (3 new, 244 baseline unchanged).
npm run build: clean.

Verification performed:
  - npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json: clean (including the new script).
  - Ran the new diagnostic script with no LLM_API_KEY configured:
    confirmed it logs "provider initialized: false" and exits cleanly,
    without crashing or leaking anything.
  - Confirmed via the test suite's captured [ai-debug] output that
    every timing/error checkpoint requested actually fires in the
    expected order (before fetch -> fetch started -> fetch resolved ->
    response status -> before/after response.json(), or the error-path
    equivalents).
